text:
For every kind of dataset:

Release settings: add the dataset to ../Publish/datasets.toml (its release tag, title, README, keywords and description), and to a topic of the website ([_site] topics there).
README: write &lt;Dataset&gt;/README.md, which becomes its page on the website, in the form of the other datasets:

# &lt;Dataset&gt;: &lt;title&gt;, and 2-4 sentences: what it is, its kind, links to the Products and Domains pages;
## Products: a table of the products, with their source (and DOI), fields (units) and domains (for records: sites, variables, time);
notes on the fields and the method, in subsections where needed;
## Precision: the precision of its fields (see Precision and file sizes);
## Licences (or ## Sources and licences): the licence of each source;
## Making the dataset, last: the commands that make it, and how to add a source.

Figures: julia fesmdata.jl figures &lt;Dataset&gt; (see Website and figures, also for the options of figure).
Page: add docs/datasets/&lt;name&gt;.qmd, which includes the README, the downloads and the figures (as docs/datasets/ghf.qmd), and add it to the menu in docs/_quarto.yml.
Release (see Releasing): tag &lt;tag&gt;-vX.Y.Z, julia fesmdata.jl stamp &lt;Dataset&gt; at the tag, and julia fesmdata.jl release &lt;Dataset&gt;. The products page and the downloads of the dataset on the website are made from the registry.

Make a folder for the source, named after it (e.g. Lucazeau2019_ghf/), with:

README.md: what the data are, where they come from, how to cite them, and their licence (check that it allows redistributing derived products before releasing them);
prepare.jl (with its own Project.toml): get the original files and write the prepared file(s) to $FESMDATA_PREPARED/&lt;source folder&gt;/ (in Julia: prepared_dir("&lt;source folder&gt;") from shared/provenance.jl).

A prepared file is a NetCDF file with:

a regular grid, given by 1D coordinate variables, either

lon, lat in degrees (units = "degrees_east", "degrees_north"): global or regional, with longitudes in -180:180 (preferred) or 0:360, in any order, and latitudes ascending or descending, evenly spaced or not (e.g. a Gaussian grid, with its cell edges from a CF bounds variable such as lat_bnds if there is one, else halfway between the latitudes); or
x, y in m or km, with the fields pointing to a grid-mapping variable (grid_mapping attribute) that holds a PROJ string in proj_params or proj4;

fields on that grid, each with units and long_name, and missing values as _FillValue or NaN. A field may have more dimensions than the grid (e.g. month, time, depth), each with a 1D coordinate variable; they are kept as they are. Variables off the grid are copied as they are, with their dimensions: on these dimensions (e.g. label(time)) or on dimensions of their own (e.g. an annual time series next to fields every 10 years, a section of the overturning streamfunction). Integer fields (e.g. masks, classes) stay integer, with flag_values and flag_meanings if they are classes. Vectors are given by their eastward and northward components, marked by the standard names eastward_&lt;name&gt; and northward_&lt;name&gt; (e.g. eastward_land_ice_surface_velocity), never by their components along the axes of a projected source grid;
optionally, fields given by region, one value per region of a Regions product (e.g. melt rates averaged over each drainage basin): a float variable on one dimension whose coordinate variable holds the region numbers, with units, long_name and the attribute regions, the Regions product (e.g. bmelt_basin(basin), regions = "BASINS-Zwally2012", basin = 1, …, 27). On each grid, every cell gets the value of its region, the field named as the dimension (basin) in &lt;GRID&gt;_&lt;regions&gt;.nc (Regions must be made first); the cells outside the regions (e.g. the open ocean beyond the shelf break, where the basins end) get the nearest region (FesmUtils extend_labels), so the field covers the whole grid and is constant over each region. They have no f_valid and are not filled or smoothed. A prepared file needs at least one field on its grid;
global attributes title, references, license and, where there is one, doi (copied to the remapped files).

Prepare the data at their own resolution: no smoothing or regridding, that is left to the remapping. Keep names and units simple (e.g. ghf in mW m-2).

Attributes
The precision of each field is in its attributes:
float z_bed(yc, xc) ;
        z_bed:units = "m" ;
        z_bed:precision = "0.01 m" ;
        z_bed:bitround_keepbits = 15 ;
precision is the tolerance ("0.01 m", "1%", "1% or 1 m yr-1", "lossless"), bitround_keepbits the mantissa bits kept (of 23; 23 is lossless).
